WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.045
Ivy Chen has two accounts with 50 euros in each.

2
00:00:06.045 --> 00:00:09.668
The bank's rule: her total must never go below zero.

3
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:15.291
Two withdrawals of 80 euros arrive at once, one in the app and one at an ATM.

4
00:00:15.291 --> 00:00:19.001
Each runs in its own transaction, at REPEATABLE READ.

5
00:00:19.233 --> 00:00:25.747
At this level each transaction reads from a snapshot: the data as it was when the transaction started.

6
00:00:25.967 --> 00:00:28.888
Each checks the rule against its snapshot.

7
00:00:28.888 --> 00:00:32.017
Each sees 100 euros, so taking 80 looks fine.

8
00:00:32.233 --> 00:00:35.084
The app takes 80 from account 109.

9
00:00:35.084 --> 00:00:37.935
The ATM takes 80 from account 209.

10
00:00:37.935 --> 00:00:40.701
Different rows, so neither waits.

11
00:00:40.933 --> 00:00:41.815
Both commit.

12
00:00:41.815 --> 00:00:46.003
Snapshot isolation only stops two writes to the same row.

13
00:00:46.233 --> 00:00:48.399
Now her total is minus 60 euros.

14
00:00:48.399 --> 00:00:52.391
Each transaction kept the rule, and together they broke it.

15
00:00:52.600 --> 00:01:00.415
This is write skew: two transactions read the same data, then each writes a different row, and neither sees the other's write.

16
00:01:00.633 --> 00:01:04.373
Now run them again at SERIALIZABLE, from 50 and 50.

17
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:08.134
Both read 100 and both take 80, as before.

18
00:01:08.134 --> 00:01:12.677
The app commits first, so account 109 is now minus 30.

19
00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:16.282
But Postgres also tracked what each one read.

20
00:01:16.282 --> 00:01:21.319
The ATM's check used the old balance of 109, which the app changed.

21
00:01:21.319 --> 00:01:25.528
The app's check used the old 209, which the ATM changed.

22
00:01:25.733 --> 00:01:29.838
Running them one after the other could never give this result.

23
00:01:29.838 --> 00:01:35.731
So when the ATM commits, Postgres refuses with a serialization failure and rolls it back.

24
00:01:35.933 --> 00:01:38.190
The ATM retries its transaction.

25
00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:46.016
The new snapshot shows a total of 20 euros, not enough for 80, so the withdrawal is refused and the rule holds.

26
00:01:46.233 --> 00:01:51.424
Only SERIALIZABLE catches write skew, so your code must be ready to retry.
